00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
hey boys and girls this is tim ferriss  and i would like to cover psychedelics  101 a few recommended resources  first for context i've spent the last 10  years heavily involved  with the scientific research as a  source of financing primarily for johns  hopkins  for imperial college in london for  research at the university of auckland  and the reason that i'm so involved with  supporting research  both into predominantly psilocybin but  also lsd  and mdma is that i believe psychedelic  compounds  hold the potential to treat treatment  resistant conditions that are very  poorly treated with current psychiatric  approaches and medications so that would  include chronic depression that would  include  chronic anxiety things like ocd  ptsd eating disorders it's a pretty long  list  most of them i would say are associated  with some degree of rigidity  or repetitive patterning and  they've changed my life certainly i'm a  case study in many respects  but i want that to be tested with  rigorous scientific research so that's  what i'm up to and i've dedicated  millions of dollars in many years to  this at this point  so let's start with books in the  category books there are a million books  about psychedelics certainly michael  pollins how to change your mind is  excellent if you want to read a teaser  of that  really the new yorker article that was  the precursor to that book the trip  treatment is a pretty short read and you  can also hear  michael pollan since he and i have had  multiple conversations on my podcast  including effectively a two-hour summary  of his book  and you can find those at 10. blog  outside of michael pollan  there are some lesser known books the  healing journey by claudio naranjo  is excellent and really talks about a  number of compounds but also combining  psychotherapeutic rappers some type of  preparation and guiding and integration  with the use of these powerful compounds  it's an outstanding  description of what can happen and what  can be done  with many of these compounds so the  healing journey by claudio naranjo  the next book i'll mention is the secret  chief which is about  leo zeph zff who was one of the best  known facilitators  and guides who conducted many many  informal empirical experiments with  many many subjects probably hundreds of  different subjects  and it's it's a really helpful window  into  what facilitation can look like  consciousness medicine  by friends suas borza b-o-u-r-z-a-t  is a newer book written by friends suas  and she  is an exceptionally experienced  facilitator who spent time with the  mazatec  indians in mexico in the state of oaxaca  and their area of expertise or areas of  expertise include  these philosophy mushrooms magic  mushrooms as they're known  which are so widely distributed now in  north america but they're really  introduced  through the mazatechs and other  indigenous peoples of that area  so francoise has a lot of experience not  only with the indigenous paths  but also with the western  psychotherapeutic models and she's very  very good at combining the two  she also has a lot of familiarity with  trauma work so i would suggest taking a  look at that  for documentaries if you want to watch  documentaries there's a lot of garbage  a few that i think are good include trip  of compassion which shows  actual session footage of mdma assisted  psychotherapy  transforming people who have complex and  treatment-resistant  ptsd post-traumatic stress disorder  whether that's from kidnapping  from bombings from any number  of different stressful events in life  that could be  inclusive of veterans victims of sexual  abuse and so on  so trip of compassion is an exceptional  movie it is low production value but  very  high impact and you just have to be  patient with it it is very intense also  i'll warn you in advance  from shock to awe covers the  healing paths of several veterans  tracing them from  opiate or i should say medication  prescription medication addiction  to use of psychedelics and the effects  that those have had on their lives  i should also say just as a quick side  note psychedelics are not a panacea they  involve very real risks  but they do have some very interesting  applications another film is  dost dos ed which  i'll leave at higher production value  and also a single primary female  protagonist  and this includes the use of iboga or  ibogaine  for opioid addiction specifically and  takes place in  british columbia and canada so those are  a few documentaries i'm sure there are  more coming they're also short video  series  by horizons which is a group best known  for its events  related to psychedelics but they've  produced some very very good  short films and videos that you can also  find on youtube  and elsewhere for researchers if you're  interested in digging into  the research or research if you prefer  you can certainly go to the johns  hopkins  center page i think it's just hopkins  psychedelic.com or dot org but if you  search johns hopkins psychedelic it will  pop right up  that is the first center for psychedelic  and consciousness research  in the united states a number of names  come to mind  include roland griffiths and matt  johnson but  the entire team there is exceptional and  they have published a lot  their studies have really been seminal  pieces of literature in  resurrecting psychedelics and creating  what people are calling the psychedelic  renaissance a lot of their work out of  johns hopkins  you have imperial college london robin  carhart harris david nutt and  others who are doing tremendous work  including some very image intensive  research with fmri i would encourage you  to take a look at  their studies there are as i mentioned  research centers or i should say  certainly serious serious research being  conducted also  at nyu new york university that includes  michael bogan shoots steve ross looking  at  psilocybin as found in so-called magic  mushrooms for alcohol use disorder  and other conditions so you can find  fantastic researchers actually now  jumping into the fray all over the u.s  there is an initiative at uc berkeley  now  and at many other institutions that are  really really  well respected for not just their work  in psychedelics but across the board  so from medical and scientific  perspective things have never been  more exciting so i'm i'm  very very thrilled to be involved with  all of that  there's certainly many thinkers many  people have written on these subjects  commented on these subjects i will say  when in doubt approach this subject area  with a high degree of skepticism  and an even higher degree of caution  you really want to measure twice and cut  once here i would not encourage  recreational use but if you have  a condition that may be well treated  that  is demonstrated in the literature to  respond well to these compounds  then it's worth doing some homework and  also  noting that most of them remain illegal  federally and otherwise  in the united states and many other  countries so i'm not  recommending that you use psychedelics  but it is a fascinating  area to study and really immerse  yourself in  if you want some of the the wackier  stuff that i find very interesting also  uh from some of the south american canon  or at least descriptions of things  in south america the wizard of the upper  amazon and singing to the plants  will give you a decent overview of what  the  animistic and sort of vegetalist  they would say vegetalismo  facets of the ayahuasca world  specifically look like  in the peruvian amazon and elsewhere in  the amazon  so with that should be plenty to start  with there's plenty more  of course you can find many podcasts on  these subjects and different compounds  on microdosing on ibogaine  specifically on my podcast the tim  ferriss show  at tim.blog forward slash podcast and  thanks for  listening  you

